-- check/bad: an `Int` where an `Int32` belongs, and the reverse. Neither
-- converts on its own (`fast-compiler.md` §3.1) and the message has to name
-- BOTH types and the two conversions, or the reader is left guessing which
-- direction they are going.
--
-- The callee is named `add` here and not `(+)`. Every operator of
-- `language.md` §6.5 desugars to a `core/Basics` value, and the reporter
-- used to name an operator on the CALLEE'S NAME ALONE — so `Int32.add`,
-- which shares a name with `+`'s desugaring and is not an operator at all,
-- was reported as "the (+) operator", an operator that does not exist on
-- this type. `List.eq` and `String.append` collided the same way. The
-- second declaration is the control: `Int32.toInt a + a` really is `+`, and
-- it is still named "(+)".
import Int32 exposing (Int32)


widen : Int32, Int -> Int32
widen a b = Int32.add a b


narrow : Int32 -> Int
narrow a = Int32.toInt a + a
